    {
      "id": "MJUe",
      "code_hash": "73900e366f365b1288d47a031ca63a60",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h1 id=\"polarization-sums-in-d-dimensions\">Polarization sums in <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(D||)</marimo-tex> dimensions</h1>\n<span class=\"paragraph\"><a href=\"/\">Browse all notebooks</a> \u00b7 <a href=\"/?file=hep/polarized_spin.py\">Massive Dirac spin states</a> \u00b7\n<a href=\"/?file=hep/qcd_gluons.py\">Gluon scattering from quarks</a></span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Query a particle by name and call <code>particle.spin_sum(p, i, j, dimension=D)</code>.\nMassless vectors have <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(D-2||)</marimo-tex> physical states; massive vectors have <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(D-1||)</marimo-tex>.\n<code>average=True</code> divides by this count. Four dimensions remain the default.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">The massless projector uses an axial reference <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(n||)</marimo-tex> with <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(p\\cdot n=\\chi\\ne0||)</marimo-tex>.\nIts <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(n^2=\\zeta||)</marimo-tex> term is retained, so the reference need not be null.\nThe massive projector includes the longitudinal Proca polarization.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">A fixed four-dimensional averaging factor is also useful when comparing\ndimensional continuations. It changes only the normalization: the projector\nstill uses <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(D||)</marimo-tex>-dimensional Lorentz slots. For example, the\n<a href=\"https://feyncalc.github.io/FeynCalcExamples/QCD/Tree/GlGl-GlGl\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">four-gluon FeynCalc reference</a>\ndivides each incoming <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(D||)</marimo-tex>-dimensional gluon sum by two.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
